Never let a cooperative guest halt -- and catch the idle halt too.

The guest is code running inside a host process, on a CPU the host owns. HLT
with interrupts disabled waits for an NMI or a reset, so a guest that reaches
it takes that core away permanently: the host cannot preempt it, the other
cores notice, and the machine bugchecks.

There are two halts, and the first version of this patch caught only one.
native_halt() is the emergency halt every panic path ends in. native_safe_halt()
is the idle halt -- default_idle() reaches it through raw_safe_halt(), and a
booted guest waits there. A guest that boots successfully and then idles would,
without this, execute a real `sti; hlt` and hang the host exactly as a
panicking one would. So both trap.

The trap is `ud2; ud2`, two in a row, and the doubling is load bearing. A
single ud2 is how WARN and BUG trap, and the host steps over a warning to keep
the boot going. It must not step over a halt. No WARN or BUG is ever followed
by a second ud2 -- there are zero `0f 0b 0f 0b` sequences in the whole image --
so the pair is a signature the host tells apart from a warning with certainty:
it stops on it and reports the guest reached idle.

Single-stepping cannot cover this case on its own. The trap flag raises #DB
after an instruction completes, and HLT does not complete; and the default idle
uses MWAIT, which a stepped guest does not pause on at all. idle=halt on the
command line routes idle through native_safe_halt so the trap is reached.

--- a/arch/x86/include/asm/irqflags.h
+++ b/arch/x86/include/asm/irqflags.h
@@ -45,8 +45,31 @@ static __always_inline void native_irq_enable(void)
 	asm volatile("sti": : :"memory");
 }

+extern int co_colinux_guest;
+
 static __always_inline void native_safe_halt(void)
 {
+	/*
+	 * The idle halt, and the one the original no-halt patch missed.
+	 *
+	 * native_halt() below is the emergency halt every panic path ends in.
+	 * This is the other one: default_idle() reaches it through
+	 * raw_safe_halt(), so it is where a booted, idle guest waits. A guest
+	 * that reaches idle and really executes `sti; hlt` takes its core away
+	 * exactly as a panicking one would -- the boot succeeding is not a
+	 * reason to let it hang the host.
+	 *
+	 * Two ud2 rather than one. A single ud2 is how WARN and BUG trap, and
+	 * the host steps over a warning; the host must not step over a halt. No
+	 * WARN is ever followed by a second ud2, so `ud2; ud2` is a signature
+	 * the host can tell apart from a warning with certainty, and it stops
+	 * on it and reports the guest reached idle.
+	 */
+	if (co_colinux_guest)
+		asm volatile("ud2; ud2");
+
 	x86_idle_clear_cpu_buffers();
 	asm volatile("sti; hlt": : :"memory");
 }
@@ -63,8 +86,11 @@ static __always_inline void native_halt(void)
  */
-extern int co_colinux_guest;
-
 static __always_inline void native_halt(void)
 {
+	/* ud2; ud2 -- the cooperative-halt signature, told apart from the
+	 * single ud2 of a WARN. See native_safe_halt above. */
 	if (co_colinux_guest)
-		asm volatile("ud2");
+		asm volatile("ud2; ud2");

 	x86_idle_clear_cpu_buffers();
 	asm volatile("hlt": : :"memory");
 }
